	{
		"name": "query_sql",
		"title": "LixRay: Query SQL",
		"description": "Run one read-only SQL statement against lix_ref (@handle/slug or UUID), optionally branch_id. Defaults to the repository default branch; no open_lix needed. Or pass context alone for its pinned branch. INSERT, UPDATE, and DELETE are rejected; use execute_sql for writes.\n\nPrefer read_files and list_files for ordinary file work; use query_sql for repository data and advanced file queries. Lix SQL is a Postgres dialect subset, not SQLite: there is no sqlite_master and no PRAGMA. Discover tables with `SELECT table_name, table_type FROM information_schema.tables WHERE table_schema = 'public' ORDER BY table_name;` and columns, with their descriptions, with `SELECT column_name, data_type, is_nullable, description FROM information_schema.columns WHERE table_schema = 'public' AND table_name = $1 ORDER BY ordinal_position;`. Core tables: lix_file (id, path, directory_id, name, content), lix_directory (id, path, parent_id, name), lix_branch, lix_commit, lix_change; entity tables are repository-defined, so discover them rather than guess. Parameters are $1, $2, … given as params: [{ kind: \"text\", value: \"…\" }] (kinds: text, int, float, json, blob). History: lix_log() lists retained commits, lix_history('lix_file') the net change per commit, lix_diff('lix_file') the working changes, lix_as_of('lix_file', $1) the state at a commit.",
